{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Take your staff and cast it before Pharaoh — let it become a תַּנִּין.</b> Here it says that Aaron should take his staff and it shall become a תַּנִּין, while earlier in parashat Shemot (Exodus 4:2) He commanded that Moses should take his own staff and it shall become a נָחָשׁ. And the commentators disagree about this: some say that תַּנִּין means a great fish, as Rashi explained on the verse ״הַתַּנִּינִם הַגְּדֹלִים״ (Genesis 1:21), and some say that תַּנִּין means a serpent, and so Rashi explained here." "Now there is room for me to explain the change of expression, and this is it: the תַּנִּין is a very large, extremely dangerous serpent, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:33) ״חֲמַת תַּנִּינִם יֵינָם״ — hear from this that its venom is greater than the venom of an ordinary serpent. Now Moses and Aaron performed all the miracles through the words of their mouth, like this serpent whose power is essentially in its mouth, and as it appears, most of the miracles were performed by means of the staffs of Moses and Aaron. And so that there be no opening for the adversary to argue and say that all the miracles were done through the power of the staffs, and perhaps they might err to say that there is an aspect of sorcery in their staffs — therefore they were turned into serpents, to say: just as the serpent bites without incantation, by its mouth, so the power of Moses and Aaron is in their mouth, and the staffs performed all those actions through the power of their spoken words, for the righteous decrees, speaks, and it is fulfilled for him. And therefore the staffs of both of them were made into serpents, as it is stated (Genesis 49:17) ״יְהִי דָן נָחָשׁ עֲלֵי דֶרֶךְ״ — so the staff of Moses and Aaron would be like serpents, burning to harm Pharaoh and all his servants. And know and understand that Moses was a shepherd and leader of Israel with the staff of his might, as a shepherd leads his flock, and he was a prince and ruler over Pharaoh as well, but Aaron ruled only over Pharaoh and his people alone. Therefore HaShem commanded that before Israel, Moses should cast his own staff and it shall become a serpent upon the road to Pharaoh and to all his people — biting the horse's heel so that its rider falls backward, an allusion to the horse of Egypt and its rider — and Moses fled from it, for he thought that his staff would forever be destructive and ruinous like a serpent, and this is not in keeping with his quality of goodness. Therefore it was said to him, ״אֱחוֹז בִּזְנָבוֹ״ — grasp it by its tail — and it became a staff in his hand, for only its face, that is, the place of the head and the mouth, is dangerous to harm and destroy the enemies of HaShem, blessed be He; behold, its tail is the opposite of this, for in the end he would shepherd Israel, doing no harm and no destruction, like the tail of a serpent which does not harm but is rather like an ordinary staff — so too this power of the serpent would become a staff in his hand, as though to say that it would be a harming serpent to the Egyptians and a staff of might to Israel simultaneously. And if you wish to say that even toward Israel it would be both staff and serpent together — a staff of might for the righteous and a burning serpent for the wicked, for the bite of the sages is the bite of a serpent — this too comes out well, that this miracle was performed before Israel specifically with the staff of Moses." "But we have not yet seen any sign that Aaron would dominate and rule over Pharaoh and all his people. Therefore HaShem commanded that before Pharaoh he should cast Aaron's staff and it shall become a תַּנִּין, whose venom is greater than the venom of an ordinary serpent — for by this He indicated that Aaron too would dominate and rule over Pharaoh, who is called תַּנִּין, as it is stated (Ezekiel 29:3) ״הַתַּנִּים הַגָּדוֹל הָרֹבֵץ בְּתוֹךְ יְאֹרָיו״. And because Pharaoh is likened to a תַּנִּין, which is greater than a serpent, therefore they showed him that Aaron's staff would become a תַּנִּין against Pharaoh, who is likened to a תַּנִּים — for an ordinary serpent cannot prevail against him so much, for the power of the תַּנִּין is greater than that of the serpent. Therefore He showed him a תַּנִּין, that his power would be greater than Pharaoh's, for regarding Pharaoh it is written ״הַתַּנִּים הַגָּדוֹל״ with a final ״ם״, indicating a female, whose power is not as great as that of the male תַּנִּין with a final ״ן״. Therefore Aaron's staff became a תַּנִּין, for it would swallow Pharaoh, who is likened to a female תַּנִּים — as it says, ״וַיִּבְלַע מַטֵּה אַהֲרֹן אֶת מַטֹּתָם״. Because the sorcerers said: 'We too have a staff of might that is likened to תַּנִּים — that is, Pharaoh and his servants — so what are you boasting about with your staff, that it should become a תַּנִּין?' Therefore He showed them that their תַּנִּינִים were weak, like females, and Aaron's staff would swallow their staffs. And there was no need to do this with Moses' staff, for this miracle had already been performed before Israel. And furthermore, because toward Israel HaShem did not want his staff to have the venom of תַּנִּינִים, and Moses' staff encompassed governance both over Israel and over Pharaoh, therefore it turned into a נָחָשׁ. But Aaron's staff, which acted only against Pharaoh, turned into a תַּנִּין, in order to nullify the power of Pharaoh, who is likened to a תַּנִּים as stated above." "And a proof for the matter is that in the plague of the Nile it says, ״וְהַמַּטֶּה אֲשֶׁר נֶהְפַּךְ לְנָחָשׁ תִּקַּח בְּיָדֶךָ״ (Exodus 7:15), and this is the staff of Moses, and it is written of it, ״הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי מַכֶּה בַּמַּטֶּה אֲשֶׁר בְּיָדִי עַל הַמַּיִם אֲשֶׁר בַּיְאֹר וְנֶהֶפְכוּ לְדָם״ (Exodus 7:17) — but its power was not so great as to destroy all the waters of Egypt and their rivers and their pools, and afterward it says, ״אֱמֹר אֶל אַהֲרֹן קַח מַטְּךָ וּנְטֵה יָדְךָ עַל מֵימֵי מִצְרַיִם עַל נַהֲרוֹתָם״ etc. (Exodus 7:19) — hear from this that the power of Aaron's staff is greater than the power of Moses' staff, for Aaron's staff acted also upon their rivers and their pools and every gathering of their waters, and this is because Aaron's staff was likened to a תַּנִּין, whose venom is greater than the venom of an ordinary serpent, while to Moses' staff no great venom was given, because he also shepherded Israel with it, and he needed to conduct himself with them gently even in a time of anger — and this is a precious allusion." "However, according to those who say that תַּנִּין means a fish, we may say that before Israel the staff turned into a serpent, to indicate that at first Israel were like a staff held erect in upright posture, and afterward their full stature was brought low to the ground to become among those who crawl in the dust. And when they grasp it by its tail — when they reach the utmost lowliness — they will return to their former state and become like this staff. And so it was: Pharaoh added servitude to them at the end, and stalked them from behind to make them like the tails of smoking firebrands, and from there they rose to their prominence. And this could not be done before Pharaoh; therefore the staff was made before him into a תַּנִּין, that is, a fish, to indicate that just as HaShem foiled his counsel at the first — for he wanted to make them like this dry staff that does not increase and multiply, and so he said ״פֶּן יִרְבֶּה״ (Exodus 1:10) — and the opposite happened, that the dry staff became a fish, as it is written (Genesis 48:16) ״וְיִדְגּוּ לָרֹב בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״ — so too now HaShem will foil his counsel. And therefore the תַּנִּין did not return to being a staff, to allude that Israel would remain in their multitude forever. And what is written, ״וַיִּבְלַע מַטֵּה אַהֲרֹן״ (Exodus 7:12) — that is the תַּנִּין that had originally been a staff. And the Nile, which contains fish, was struck by means of Moses' staff specifically, which had originally been a serpent, but Aaron's staff, which had originally been a fish and now became a staff, not in Pharaoh's presence — it would not be fitting that it should kill their fish after it itself had been a fish. And afterward He commanded to take Aaron's staff to strike all the rivers and pools, and therefore it says ״וְהַדָּגָה אֲשֶׁר בַּיְאֹר מֵתָה״ (Exodus 7:21) — but not that which was in the pools and rivers, which were struck by means of Aaron's staff. And He was harsher regarding the Nile because it was their god; therefore He executed judgments upon their god, and showed them that it could not save the fish. But the pools and rivers did not lay claim to divinity; therefore He did not kill their fish."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:06.222058750Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}